# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values — a leftover placeholder
# becomes the actual setting (e.g. an empty DB_PASSWORD is a real, working
# connection attempt with a blank password, not a helpful error). Both
# `.env` and `.env*.local` are gitignored; never commit real secrets.
#
# `.env` rather than `.env.local` because docker-compose auto-loads only
# `.env`, while Next.js reads either — one file means both see the same
# values. If you do use `.env.local`, compose needs `--env-file .env.local`
# passed explicitly. See the env file section of README.md.

# From Google Cloud Console > APIs & Services > Credentials
# Authorized redirect URI must be: <NEXTAUTH_URL>/api/auth/callback/google
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=

# Comma-separated Google sign-in emails that always have admin access
# (/admin — manage the social handles directory, add/remove other admins).
# Permanent and can't be revoked through the app itself, unlike admins added
# via the UI (stored in the `admins` table instead) — see lib/admin.ts.
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# Base URL of the app (e.g. http://localhost:3000 in dev)
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# MySQL database (see database/schema.sql / lib/db.ts). What these point to
# depends on which box this file is on:
#
# - Dev/staging (docker-compose.yaml): the `node` service reaches the shared
#   `master-sql` container over the `shared-net` Docker network and
#   overrides DB_HOST/DB_PORT itself (see that file). The values below are
#   only used by `npm run dev` on the bare host — point them at whatever
#   MySQL you're actually reaching locally.
# - Production (Vercel/Render): a free managed MySQL-compatible database —
#   see DEPLOYMENT.md, which recommends TiDB Cloud Serverless. Reached over
#   the public network with SSL required, e.g.:
#
#     DB_HOST=gateway01.<region>.prod.aws.tidbcloud.com
#     DB_PORT=4000
#     DB_USER=<prefix>.root
#     DB_PASSWORD=<generated password>
#     DB_NAME=lmc
#     DB_SSL=true
#
#   Set these in Vercel/Render's own environment-variable dashboard, not in a
#   file — see DEPLOYMENT.md. No IP-allowlist step is needed with this kind
#   of provider (unlike the old DigitalOcean-cluster setup): it authenticates
#   by credentials over TLS, which is required since Vercel's build/runtime
#   IPs aren't fixed.
DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
DB_PORT=3306
DB_USER=root
DB_PASSWORD=
DB_NAME=lmc
DB_SSL=false
